                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Hunter of Antiques has one thing going for it, the presentation. \nFPS Creator games always have a really moody atmosphere due to the engine's ambient occlusion effect, which looks great even by today's standards. With high resolution textures and some well placed lighting, this game is probably visually the apex of FPSC games. The music adds a lot too, enabling the devs to hide the lack of detail in every map.\n\nUnfortunately, the engine's limitations are ultimately what hold the game back. This is a puzzle/platformer game, where one misstep can kill you. However loading screens can take upwards of 30+ seconds, which is simply too long for a game like this. Unity would load the same map in less than 4 seconds easily. \n\nThe game has no auto-save system either, so if you start to read a book or catch up on your favorite TV show, find your soulmate, get married etc during the excruciatingly long load times and accidentally forget about saving, you will start over from the beginning. Not to mention, since you can raise a child during all the loading, it's easy to forget what to do during certain platforming sections, resulting in even more deaths, and even more loading screens. By the time you finish this game, your kid is probably already in college.",
